{"candidates":[{"content":"Replace the Boolean status assignment with a finite distributive lattice of epistemic grounding levels. The transition operator is monotone over the lattice product; the grounded extension is the least fixed point projected to Boolean acceptance. Determinism and polynomial cost hold because the lattice is finite and the operator is polynomial-time computable. Reinstatement is a derived property of the lattice order: a defended argument's ground level is at least the meet of its defenders' levels. Criticizable: the choice of lattice levels is external and may smuggle in preference; root validity must be verified for the projection.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"},{"role":"dependence","target":"8b9fe36aa883ebfc26e615fcfe654dcddd265c98d0942985afd089e97cc937e4"}],"typicality":0.35},{"content":"Model warrant status as the least fixed point of a monotone operator over a free idempotent semiring whose elements are minimal attacker constellations. The grounded extension is the image under a homomorphism that maps nonempty constellations to UNDECIDED. This makes the suspension boundary an algebraic invariant rather than a post-hoc diagnostic, and yields polynomial algorithms via semiring path problems. Criticizable: semiring operations may not capture defense depth unless the carrier is chosen carefully; the polynomiality claim depends on the semiring satisfying distributivity.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"986d93e07b358fa8d689939883733eb3799f1d998665b237c764ba14fbaf9486"},{"role":"dependence","target":"b25c49bfd4585cd0824221f7fd6fa154ffed828ad96ef4adc25fe3bed6b8517c"}],"typicality":0.25},{"content":"Add an explicit second-order rule: an argument is accepted iff all its attackers are rejected, and rejected iff at least one accepted attacker exists; but with a guarded rule that also tracks 'unresolved attacker' in a three-valued logic. This reaxiomatizes the grounded extension in the style of well-founded semantics, where the grounded extension is the well-founded model. The expansion allows adding well-founded negative premises from support chains without leaving the polynomial class via alternating fixpoint. Determinism and reinstatement follow from the well-founded semantics. Criticizable: this may duplicate existing closure-rule work and requires proving equivalence with the current Pass 1 fixed point for standard frameworks.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"c08594c10a414309047f9fa543ead03f028bb6260264dafc3e784e8567cc477d"},{"role":"dependence","target":"c96a52fc060980a4742f93c9719f67bcaf0e3e60d1fd0d9f3a0d39c88af8caba"}],"typicality":0.5},{"content":"Parameterize grounded semantics by a well-founded partial order on warrants. Each attack edge is effective only if the attacking warrant is not strictly dominated by a defending warrant that is accepted; otherwise it is ignored. For each fixed order, the resulting framework's grounded extension is deterministic and polynomial. Reinstatement remains derived because the order is static. This expands the semantics to priority-based conflicts without abandoning skepticism. Criticizable: the source of the order is unconstrained; if the order is not well-founded or inconsistent, the guarantees fail; this may be a preference-based argumentation variant rather than an extension of the original.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"}],"typicality":0.45},{"content":"Develop an incremental algorithm that maintains the grounded extension under single-edge additions and deletions using a memoized affected region and local re-computation of the least fixed point. The semantics is unchanged, but the expansion is in operational strength: amortized polynomial updates. This is valuable for interactive conjecture refinement where edges are added as warrants evolve. Criticizable: worst-case complexity may degrade; memory overhead of dependency tracking is nontrivial; the proposal does not expand the expressiveness of the semantics.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"b25c49bfd4585cd0824221f7fd6fa154ffed828ad96ef4adc25fe3bed6b8517c"}],"typicality":0.6},{"content":"Introduce a warrant-provenance monad into the fixpoint iteration: each accepted or rejected status carries a proof term in a DAG of warrants. The proof terms are generated compositionally and are polynomial-size. This turns the grounded extension into a proof-carrying artifact, enabling auditing of committed roots without altering the accepted set. The expansion preserves all guarantees because the status projection is unchanged. Criticizable: proof-term explosion in practice; the proof system must be shown sound and complete for the fixed point; 'validity of every committed root' becomes a proof-checking problem rather than a semantic guarantee.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"986d93e07b358fa8d689939883733eb3799f1d998665b237c764ba14fbaf9486"}],"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}